talk(558,'1982-04-05','Mariusz Wodzicki','P001018-P001023','Local index densities and spectral boundary conditions',r'The actual notes run from the heading on P001019 through P001021. Wodzicki reviews spectral zeta functions, local heat coefficients and the signature theorem, then explains the nonlocal boundary correction and spectral boundary conditions. Gelfand asks about additive invariants. The notebook mixes a closed-manifold formula with a boundary hypothesis and sketches a normal, rather than necessarily self-adjoint, tangential operator; these scopes are kept separate.')
item(558,'P001019','theorem','The index as an integral of a local density',r'For an elliptic differential system \(D\), possibly on a manifold with boundary, \(\operatorname{ind}D=\int_M\alpha_0\) is attributed to Atiyah--Singer.',r'For an elliptic differential operator \(D:C^\infty(E)\to C^\infty(F)\) on a closed compact manifold, the Fredholm index equals the integral of the constant coefficient in the local heat supertrace. On a manifold with boundary one must first specify an elliptic boundary realization; its formula generally includes boundary terms.',r'This is an established index/heat-kernel theorem; \citet[Chapter 1, \S1.7]{gilkey1984invariance} gives the analytic formulation, while \citet[Theorem (3.10)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} exhibit the boundary correction. A bare differential expression on a manifold with boundary does not have the asserted Fredholm index.',('gilkey1984invariance','atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1'),literal='false as written',reconstruction='We allow different source and target bundles and separate the closed case from boundary realizations.')
item(558,'P001019','theorem','Meromorphic continuation of a positive elliptic zeta function',r'For \(A=D^*D\) or \(DD^*\), poles are listed at \((n-j)/m\), with none at \(0,-1,-2,\ldots\).',r'Let \(A\) be a positive self-adjoint elliptic differential operator of order \(m>0\) on a closed \(n\)-manifold, with zero eigenvalues omitted. Then \(\zeta_A(s)=\sum_{\lambda>0}\lambda^{-s}\) converges for \(\operatorname{Re}s>n/m\), extends meromorphically with at most simple poles at \((n-j)/m\), and is regular at nonpositive integers. Some candidate poles vanish. General classical pseudodifferential operators need a more qualified statement.',r'This is classical complex-power/heat asymptotics: \citet{seeley1968powers} is the 1968 Russian translation of earlier work, and \citet[\S1.10]{gilkey1984invariance} supplies the heat formulation. It continues the expository item 1972-296.1.',('seeley1968powers','gilkey1984invariance'),reconstruction='Closedness, differential order, positivity, omission of the kernel and ``possible'' poles are essential additions.')
item(558,'P001019','claim','The shifted-zeta formula for the index',r'\(\operatorname{ind}D=\zeta_{1+D^*D}(0)-\zeta_{1+DD^*}(0)\).',r'For a closed elliptic Fredholm realization, the positive eigenvalues of \(D^*D\) and \(DD^*\) agree with multiplicity. Therefore for every \(\varepsilon>0\), \[\zeta_{\varepsilon+D^*D}(s)-\zeta_{\varepsilon+DD^*}(s)=\varepsilon^{-s}\operatorname{ind}D\] first in a convergent half-plane and then by continuation; evaluation at zero gives the notebook formula.',r'The equality is an elementary spectral-pairing consequence of the heat-index method, as in \citet[\S1.7]{gilkey1984invariance}. It avoids the missing-kernel problem of unshifted zeta functions.',('gilkey1984invariance',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The displayed all-s identity is our explanatory derivation, not a second formula claimed to be in the notes.')
item(558,'P001020','method','Local zeta values from a parameter-dependent parametrix',r'Equation (5) integrates \(b^\circ_{-m-n}(x,\xi,\lambda)\) over \(|\xi|=1\) and a ray, with a minus sign and factor \(1/m\); it is a density.',r'For an invertible positive elliptic differential \(A\), if \(\operatorname{tr}K_A(t;x,x)\sim\sum_j a_j(x)t^{(j-n)/m}\), then \(\zeta_A(0;x)=a_n(x)\). If zero modes are omitted, subtract the kernel-projection density. The coefficient is computed from the homogeneous degree \(-m-n\) resolvent symbol, using an admissible ray and a fixed convention for \((A-\lambda)^{-1}\), Fourier factors and contour orientation.',r'The heat version is verified in \citet[\S1.10]{gilkey1984invariance}. The parametrix expression belongs to the Seeley/Wodzicki calculus; the notebook alone does not fix enough conventions to certify a sign error in Seeley. The stronger assertion of cut-independent pointwise density is not valid for arbitrary pseudodifferential operators; compare \citet[Remark 4.4]{ponge2006spectral}.',('gilkey1984invariance','seeley1968powers','ponge2006spectral'),match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='We supply a convention-independent heat statement and preserve the resolvent formula as a separate, convention-sensitive notebook assertion.')
item(558,'P001020','construction','The signature operator and its chirality splitting',r'The notes introduce \(D=(d+d^*)|_{\Omega_+}\), with a phase times the Hodge star, and identify its index with the signature.',r'On a closed oriented Riemannian manifold of dimension \(4k\), the Hodge-star phase defines the standard chirality involution on complex differential forms; \(d+d^*\) interchanges its eigensubbundles. The index of its positive-to-negative part, with the compatible chirality convention, is the signature of the middle-dimensional intersection pairing.',r'This is the classical signature operator. \citet[Chapter 3, \S3.1]{gilkey1984invariance} fixes the chirality convention; \citet[\S4]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} explain the boundary modification. The preceding scanned hypothesis \(\partial M\ne\varnothing\) cannot accompany the uncorrected integral equality.',('gilkey1984invariance','atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1'),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The notebook phase uses an insufficiently specified n; we do not silently replace it by a different exponent. The modern statement specifies the standard chirality by its operator properties.')
item(558,'P001020','claim','The div–grad–curl example and its missing sign',r'Gelfand writes \(\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}0&\operatorname{div}\\\operatorname{grad}&\operatorname{rot}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)^2=\Delta I\), with a parenthetical \(\mathbb R^4\).',r'Using the usual Euclidean operators on \(\mathbb R^3\), the literal square is \(\operatorname{diag}(\Delta,2\operatorname{grad}\operatorname{div}-\Delta I_3)\). A self-adjoint Dirac-type version is \[B=\begin{pmatrix}0&-\operatorname{div}\\\operatorname{grad}&\operatorname{curl}\end{pmatrix},\qquad B^2=-\Delta I_4.\] The four refers to a scalar plus three vector components, not a four-dimensional base.',r'\citet[Introduction, p.~45]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} discuss the same operator only up to sign conventions. Our vector-calculus calculation and the repeated \(\mathbb R^3\) wording on P001024 settle the notebook ambiguity; the accompanying symbolic check is included.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),literal='false as written',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='We explicitly fix the conventional Euclidean signs instead of treating the shorthand as a literal self-adjoint matrix identity.')
item(558,'P001020','theorem','Local signature and Todd densities',r'Atiyah--Bott--Patodi identify \(\alpha_0\) with \(L_k(p)\), in dimension four \(p_1/3\); for \(\bar\partial\), a Kähler assumption is stated for the Todd density.',r'For the geometric signature operator the constant heat supertrace density is the top-degree Hirzebruch \(L\)-form. For the Dolbeault complex with the usual Kähler metric and compatible Hermitian connection, the local index density is the appropriate Todd form (and the Chern character for coefficients). Equality of integrated characteristic numbers does not make arbitrary pointwise representatives identical.',r'\citet{atiyahbottpatodi1973heat} is the 1973 publication named by the talk. \citet[Chapter 3, \S\S3.1 and 3.6--3.7]{gilkey1984invariance} provides the precise geometric versions. The notebook correctly warns against dropping the Kähler qualification in its stated pointwise formula.',('atiyahbottpatodi1973heat','gilkey1984invariance'),literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(558,'P001020','theorem','The signature theorem with its eta boundary correction',r'\(\operatorname{sign}M=\int_M L_k(p)-\eta(\partial M)\), for a product metric near the boundary.',r'For compact oriented Riemannian \(M^{4k}\), product near \(Y=\partial M\), define the signature on the image of \(H^{2k}(M,Y;\mathbb R)\to H^{2k}(M;\mathbb R)\). With the APS odd-signature operator on even forms and its boundary orientation, \[\operatorname{sign}M=\int_M L-\eta(B_Y;0).\] This normalization is different from writing \((\eta+h)/2\) for a general Dirac boundary problem.',r'\citet[Theorem (4.14)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} is the exact result. Its announcement appeared in 1973, the full paper was received 25 March 1974, and publication followed in 1975; the notebook’s ``1974'' should not replace those distinct dates.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1','atiyahpatodisinger1973spectral'),literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(558,'P001020','question','Is signature the only additive invariant?',r'Gelfand asks whether signature is the unique additive invariant of manifolds with boundary.',r'“Additive” has no specified gluing category or normalization here. Under gluing compact even-dimensional manifolds along complete closed odd-dimensional boundary components, Euler characteristic is also additive, since \(\chi(M\cup_YN)=\chi(M)+\chi(N)-\chi(Y)\) and \(\chi(Y)=0\). It is independent of signature: \(\chi(D^{4k})=1\), \(\sigma(D^{4k})=0\). If orientation-oddness or cobordism invariance is imposed, that is a different question.',r'\citet[Chapter 1, Corollary (1.4)]{karraskreckneumannossa1973cutting} already classify the corresponding real-valued cut-and-paste invariants of closed oriented manifolds by Euler characteristic and signature. This is useful context, not an exact match to an unstated boundary gluing law.',('karraskreckneumannossa1973cutting',),cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',reconstruction='The Euler-characteristic example refutes only the plainly stated broad interpretation; no extra axiom is attributed to Gelfand.',status='The broad uniqueness interpretation is false, while the intended restricted axioms cannot be recovered from this line.',confidence='medium',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(558,'P001020','claim','Filling independence and nonlocality of the signature defect',r'Doubling shows that \(\eta(\partial M)\) is independent of the filling; lens spaces show it is nonlocal because it fails multiplicativity under coverings.',r'For two product-metric fillings of the same oriented metric boundary, glue one to the orientation reverse of the other and apply the closed signature theorem and signature additivity. Their defects \(\int L-\sigma\) agree. A universal natural local boundary-density integral would multiply under finite local-isometry coverings; the signature eta invariant need not do so.',r'\citet[Introduction, pp.~43--45]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} give exactly this motivation and the sphere/lens-space obstruction. Filling independence does not say eta is independent of the boundary metric.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Two possibly different fillings, common boundary metric and orientation reversal make the gluing argument precise.')
item(558,'P001021','construction','Spectral boundary projections and the normal-operator qualification',r'Near the boundary \(D=\partial_u+A\), with \(A\) normal and spectrum outside a cone near \(i\mathbb R\); \(P_A\) projects to \(\operatorname{Re}\lambda\ge0\). Local elliptic boundary conditions need not exist.',r'The classical APS construction assumes \(D=\sigma(\partial_u+A)\) in a product collar, where \(\sigma:E|_Y\to F|_Y\) is an isomorphism and \(A\) is self-adjoint elliptic on \(E|_Y\). Requiring \(P_{\ge0}(A)(f|_Y)=0\) gives an elliptic Fredholm realization. More general sectorial projections require symbol cuts and resolvent estimates; normality plus a bare spectral drawing is not a complete analytic hypothesis list.',r'\citet[\S2 and Theorem (3.10)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} verify the self-adjoint case. \citet[\S3 and Appendix A]{ponge2006spectral} provide the modern spectral-cut framework, not by themselves a proof of this entire normal-boundary index assertion.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1','ponge2006spectral'),match='partial',literal='not determinable',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The modern statement deliberately separates the proved classical boundary theorem from the notebook’s larger normal-operator claim.')
item(558,'P001021','theorem','APS index formula and the role of the zero modes',r'\(\operatorname{ind}D=\int\alpha_0-(h_0+\eta(A))/2\); \(h_0\) counts purely imaginary eigenvalues, while the notes explicitly say APS used self-adjoint \(A\).',r'Under the classical self-adjoint product-collar assumptions, \[\begin{aligned}\operatorname{ind}D_{\mathrm{APS}}&=\int_M\alpha_0-\frac{\eta_A(0)+\dim\ker A}{2},\\ \eta_A(s)&=\sum_{\lambda\ne0}\operatorname{sign}(\lambda)|\lambda|^{-s}.\end{aligned}\] The choice to exclude nonnegative boundary eigenmodes determines the kernel term; changing the zero-mode boundary subspace changes the index.',r'\citet[Theorem (3.10)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} proves this formula. The normal-operator expression using \(\operatorname{Re}\lambda\) is a proposed extension with only a partial publication match here. It is not silently substituted into the original APS theorem.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),match='partial',literal='not determinable',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The precise theorem assumes self-adjointness. The manuscript’s distinction is retained.')
item(558,'P001021','claim','Variation of the signature eta invariant on a cylinder',r'\(\int_{N\times I}L_k(p)=\eta_{p_1}-\eta_{p_2}\) is written below a cylinder diagram.',r'For an oriented cylinder of dimension \(4k\), with product metrics at both ends, its signature is zero. Applying the signature boundary theorem yields that its \(L\)-integral equals the signed difference of the two boundary eta invariants, with signs determined by outward boundary orientation. For a general family of self-adjoint elliptic operators, an analogous formula requires the spectral-flow/kernel terms.',r'The signature case follows directly from \citet[Theorem (4.14)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1}. The source says only “elliptic operator” at this point; a general family formula cannot be inferred by omitting integer jumps.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),match='partial',reconstruction='The theorem is restricted to the geometric signature situation actually illustrated; the indices p or rho in the manuscript are not interpreted as additional data.')
corr('P001019','Index formula with a possible boundary','M possibly has boundary, followed by ind D=integral alpha0','Assume M is closed for this equality, or specify a boundary realization and correction.','Ellipticity of the differential expression alone does not give a Fredholm problem with boundary.')
corr('P001020','Hypothesis preceding the uncorrected signature formula',r'\partial M\ne\varnothing',r'\partial M=\varnothing for formulas (6)--(7) without a boundary term','The scan shows the nonempty-boundary sign; formula (11) later introduces the necessary eta correction.')
corr('P001020','div–grad–curl base dimension',r'In R^4',r'On R^3, acting on a scalar plus a three-component vector','The same example explicitly says R^3 on P001024; standard curl in this matrix is three-dimensional.','ambiguity')
corr('P001020;P001024','Square of the vector-calculus matrix','All positive div, grad, curl entries square to Delta I','With conventional operators use upper-right −div and obtain −Delta I; the displayed all-positive matrix has vector block 2 grad div−Delta.','Independent symbolic multiplication and curl curl=grad div−Delta verify the correction.')
corr('P001021','Boundary restriction in (15)',r'P_A(f|_A)=0',r'P_A(f|_Y)=0, Y=\partial M','The scan genuinely uses A as the restriction subscript, but A is the operator, not the boundary.')
corr('P001021-P001022','Normal tangential operator attributed to APS','A normal; eta defined through real parts of eigenvalues','Classical APS assumes self-adjoint A; retain the normal extension as a separate assertion with additional hypotheses to be identified.','The scan itself says APS used self-adjoint A; APS I Theorem (3.10) confirms this scope.','ambiguity')

talk(559,'1982-04-12','Mariusz Wodzicki','P001021-P001025','Eta regularity, spectral cuts, and local zeta invariants',r'The actual heading is on P001022. The first part repeats the spectral boundary problem and discusses global versus local residues. Gelfand asks why self-adjointness and a filling should be needed. Wodzicki then compares zeta functions defined by two spectral cuts, relates their integer values to local residues, and writes a shifted-zeta formula with a missing power of the shift. The final p-adic analogy is not sufficiently specified to identify an automorphic theorem.')
item(559,'P001022','construction','The collar operator and boundary bundle identification',r'\(D:E\to F\) is written as \(\partial_u+A\), while \(A:E|_Y\to F|_Y\) is then assigned eigenspaces.',r'Factor the invertible normal symbol: \(D=\sigma(\partial_u+A)\). Then \(A\) is an operator on the single boundary bundle \(E|_Y\), after identifying the target by \(\sigma^{-1}\). Its nonnegative spectral projection can act on \(f|_Y\). Without this identification, an operator between distinct bundles has no intrinsic spectrum.',r'This factorization is explicit in \citet[Theorem (3.10)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1}. The notebook suppresses \(\sigma\), a harmless shorthand only if the bundle identification is supplied.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),reconstruction='The missing normal-symbol isomorphism is supplied, not read from the scan.')
item(559,'P001022','theorem','The repeated APS formula and the choice of projection',r'\(P_+f|_Y=0\) and \(\operatorname{ind}D=\int\alpha_0-(h+\eta)/2\) are repeated for normal \(A\).',r'For self-adjoint \(A\), the displayed \(-h/2\) formula corresponds to \(P_{\ge0}\), not the strictly positive projection alone. For \(P_{>0}\), allowing the kernel changes the index by \(h\). The normal case requires an explicitly chosen imaginary-axis spectral subspace and additional analytic hypotheses.',r'\citet[Theorem (3.10)]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} verifies the nonnegative convention. This record is a genuine repeated occurrence of 1981-558.11--12; it should remain linked rather than counted as a second independent discovery.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='We distinguish positive from nonnegative and do not claim to have proved the full normal-operator extension.')
item(559,'P001023','method','Heat supertrace as the basis of the boundary index proof',r'Equation (9) writes \(\operatorname{ind}D=\operatorname{Tr}e^{-tD^*D}-\operatorname{Tr}e^{-tDD^*}\).',r'For a closed densely defined Fredholm operator with compact-resolvent nonnegative realizations \(D^*D\) and \(DD^*\), positive eigenvalues pair under \(D\), so their heat-trace difference equals the index for every \(t>0\). For a boundary problem, \(D^*\) must be the adjoint of the chosen APS realization, with its corresponding complementary boundary condition.',r'\citet[\S3]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} use this identity in the boundary calculation. The formula is older than the talk and is not valid for two independently chosen boundary domains.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),reconstruction='The operator domains are indispensable supplied hypotheses.')
item(559,'P001023','theorem','Positive spectral zeta: a zero global residue can have a nonzero local density',r'\(\zeta_+(s)=\sum_{\lambda>0}\lambda^{-s}=\operatorname{Tr}(P_+A^{-s})\) has a finite value at zero, although its local density may have a residue whose integral is zero.',r'For a self-adjoint classical elliptic operator of positive order on a closed manifold, \(\zeta_+(s)=\operatorname{Tr}(P_+|A|^{-s})\) is regular at zero. The meromorphic diagonal trace density can have a nonzero pointwise residue; its integral vanishes. Thus regularity of a global trace does not establish pointwise holomorphy.',r'\citet[Theorem 0.1 and Introduction]{gilkey1981residue} establish global eta regularity in all dimensions and discuss nonzero local residues. Together with zeta regularity this gives the positive part. \citet[Theorem 4.3 and Remarks 4.4--4.5]{ponge2006spectral} explain the residue of projections. Gilkey’s 1981 publication precedes this talk.',('gilkey1981residue','ponge2006spectral'),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='We use absolute values on the spectral powers and specify closedness and classical order.')
item(559,'P001023','definition','The sum and difference of the two spectral halves',r'\(\zeta(A)=\zeta_+(A)+\zeta_-(A)\), \(\eta(A)=\zeta_+(A)-\zeta_-(A)\).',r'For real nonzero spectrum define \(\zeta_\pm(s)=\sum_{\pm\lambda>0}|\lambda|^{-s}\). Then \(\zeta_{|A|}(s)=\zeta_+(s)+\zeta_-(s)\) and \(\eta_A(s)=\zeta_+(s)-\zeta_-(s)\). A spectral-cut zeta function of \(A\), rather than \(|A|\), has a phase on the negative part. At zero those phases can be discarded only after checking the regularity just discussed.',r'The distinction is explicit in \citet[\S4, equations (4.1)--(4.2)]{ponge2006spectral}. The notebook writes values at zero; extending its first equality to all \(s\) without \(|A|\) would be false.',('ponge2006spectral',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='We separate functions from their regularized values at zero and make the hidden absolute-value convention explicit.')
item(559,'P001023','question','Boundary values for unspecified general characteristic classes',r'Gelfand cites Gabrielov, Gelfand and Fuks for algebraic reasons for nonlocality and says the analogous task for general classes has not been done.',r'The class of characteristic classes, geometric structures, equivalence relation on boundary data and desired invariant are unspecified. The sentence does not determine a unique generalization of the signature eta invariant.',r'The adjacent Pontryagin discussion suggests characteristic-class transgression, but is not enough to identify a theorem. The well-known Gabrielov--Gelfand--Losik papers must not be silently substituted for the name \ru{Фукс} actually visible in the scan; the attribution remains unresolved.',(),cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',status='No precise new open problem or exact later solution can be assigned without additional source evidence.',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(559,'P001023','remark','Stredder and Epstein: an identifiable bibliographic pointer',r'Two papers by Stredder and Epstein are listed as J. Differential Geometry 10 (1975), 645, about natural differential operators.',r'The identifiable works are Epstein, “Natural tensors on Riemannian manifolds”, pp.~631--645, and Stredder, “Natural differential operators on Riemannian manifolds and representations of the orthogonal and special orthogonal groups”, pp.~647--660. No particular classification theorem is stated in the seminar notes.',r'The two titles and page ranges are independently recorded in the reference list of \citet{fegansteer2005second}. The original DOI/Project Euclid routes did not open in this run, so the two original entries are marked unverified and the verification level remains indirect. Page 645 is the end of Epstein’s paper, not a common starting page.',('epstein1975natural','stredder1975natural','fegansteer2005second'),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',status='The bibliographic pointer is identified; no full-text verification of either original classification is claimed.')
item(559,'P001023-P001024','question','Is self-adjointness necessary for spectral asymmetry?',r'Gelfand asks \ru{зачем самосопряженность A?}; the subsequent construction uses complex spectral cuts.',r'For a positive-order classical elliptic operator on a closed manifold, complex powers and a zeta function can be defined without self-adjointness when a ray avoids both the principal-symbol spectrum and the operator spectrum and satisfies the required parameter-dependent resolvent estimates. Replacing a real-sign eta sum requires specifying the cut or sector; it is not the same definition without choices.',r'Seeley’s complex-power theory predates the talk; \citet{seeley1968powers} is an early published translation. \citet[\S\S2--4]{ponge2006spectral} give the later precise asymmetry comparison. Wodzicki’s relevant papers appeared in February 1982 and February 1984; the closed-manifold construction is not an unresolved question of principle.',('seeley1968powers','wodzicki1982spectral','wodzicki1984local','ponge2006spectral'),problem='GP-1981-44',match='partial',reconstruction='We answer the closed-operator functional-calculus question precisely. The broader normal boundary index assertion in 1981-559.2 is not proved by this answer.',status='Self-adjointness is unnecessary for admissible-cut complex powers. The choice dependence and boundary-domain issues must be specified separately.',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
item(559,'P001023','question','Does eta regularity require a filling?',r'An eta invariant is described through a filling and the notes say the case without a filling has not been proved.',r'For every self-adjoint classical elliptic operator of positive order on a closed manifold, in any dimension, the eta function has a meromorphic continuation regular at zero. Its spectral definition needs no bounding manifold. A geometric filling can give an index formula for its value but is not required for existence.',r'\citet[Theorem 0.1]{gilkey1981residue} proves the general regularity, following the earlier odd-dimensional APS result. The notebook sentence is false if read as a claim about all such self-adjoint operators; it may instead refer to the unspecified nonself-adjoint extension. Earlier occurrences are 1972-296.2--3 and 1972-296.5.',('gilkey1981residue',),literal='false as written',problem='GP-1981-43',match='partial',reconstruction='The ambiguity between self-adjoint regularity and the normal extension is retained rather than resolved by attribution.',status='The self-adjoint no-filling problem was solved before this talk. The intended broader hypothesis set is not recoverable.',confidence='medium')
item(559,'P001023','claim','A shifted local zeta representative of the index density',r'\(\alpha_0(x)=\zeta(D^*D+\varepsilon,x)-\zeta(DD^*+\varepsilon,x)\).',r'For a closed elliptic operator, the difference of shifted local zeta values at zero integrates to its index. It equals the constant term in \(e^{-\varepsilon t}\) times the local heat supertrace. If negative-power supertrace coefficients vanish pointwise, as in the classical geometric Dirac case, this is the usual local index density; for a general elliptic operator the chosen local representative can depend on the shift.',r'The heat-to-zeta relation in \citet[\S\S1.7 and 1.10]{gilkey1984invariance} and the spectral pairing in 1981-558.3 give this exact interpretation. Equality of integrals should not be strengthened into equality of all local representatives.',('gilkey1984invariance',),match='partial',reconstruction='We distinguish the shifted representative from a separately fixed canonical density and supply the suppressed evaluation at s=0.')
item(559,'P001024','method','The corrected ray integral and the alleged Seeley sign error',r'Equation (15) has \(+1/m\); equation (16), labelled correct, has \(-e^{i\theta}/m\) and \(b^\circ_{-m-n}(x,\xi,e^{i\theta}\lambda)\). The margin says Seeley has the wrong sign.',r'The substitution from a ray variable \(z=e^{i\theta}\lambda\) contributes the factor \(e^{i\theta}\). A meaningful comparison must also fix whether the resolvent is \((A-z)^{-1}\) or \((z-A)^{-1}\), the orientation, trace/Fourier normalization and any kernel term. The scan supports the corrected minus sign and a zero first argument of the local zeta value, but does not by itself establish a publication error.',r'\citet[\S2]{ponge2006spectral} state explicit complex-power and residue conventions. The original Seeley formula was not verified at this level in this run; the attribution of an error is consequently an unresolved lead rather than an endorsed correction to his paper.',('seeley1968powers','ponge2006spectral'),literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',reconstruction='Only the ray substitution is convention-independent; the full formula is not silently normalized to agree with a modern source.')
item(559,'P001024','claim','The repeated div–grad–curl square',r'The all-positive vector-calculus matrix is now explicitly on \(\mathbb R^3\), but is again said to square to \(\Delta I\).',r'With standard Euclidean conventions, its vector block squares to \(2\operatorname{grad}\operatorname{div}-\Delta I_3\), not \(\Delta I_3\). Replacing the upper-right entry by \(-\operatorname{div}\) gives the self-adjoint elliptic matrix whose square is \(-\Delta I_4\).',r'This is the same occurrence as the example in 1981-558.6, repeated a week later. The algebraic calculation is included with its independent polynomial-symbol check; \citet[Introduction]{atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1} is the historical geometric context.',('atiyahpatodisinger1975spectral1',),literal='false as written',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='The correction fixes a declared convention; the scan is transcribed faithfully.')
item(559,'P001024-P001025','theorem','Differences of spectral-cut zeta functions at integer points',r'For two rays of minimal growth, \(\rho(s)=\zeta_\theta(A,s)-\zeta_{\theta^{\prime}}(A,s)\) is meromorphic; its residues vanish microlocally at integral candidate poles and its integral-point values are local.',r'Let \(P\) be a positive integer-order \(m\) classical elliptic operator on a closed manifold with two admissible spectral cuts, and let \(\Pi_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}(P)\) be the associated sectorial projection. With arguments in \((\theta-2\pi,\theta)\), \[\rho(s)=(1-e^{-2\pi i s})\operatorname{TR}(\Pi_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}P_\theta^{-s}).\] The factor cancels the possible simple pole at any integer \(k\), and \[\rho(k)=\frac{2\pi i}{m}\operatorname{Res}(\Pi_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}P^{-k}).\] The residue is an integral of a local symbol density.',r'\citet[Theorem 4.2, equations (4.13)--(4.14)]{ponge2006spectral} prove this precise formulation and trace the method to Wodzicki. The 1982 paper was already published before the talks; the fuller 1984 treatment followed them. The primary Wodzicki publisher records were opened, but the exact original theorem text was unavailable, so priority between those two articles is not inferred from the notebook.',('wodzicki1982spectral','wodzicki1984local','ponge2006spectral'),cls='A',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The canonical-trace notation and orientation convention are modern; the notebook’s sign in its symbol-residue formula is not compared without matching conventions.',status='The integer-value residue formula is established in the stated classical calculus; the microscopic cancellation and global integral are distinct statements.')
item(559,'P001025','claim','Cut independence at zero and the nonpositive-integer extension',r'Part (d) says \(\rho(\ell)=0\) for \(\ell=0,-1,-2,\ldots\), while the following hint mentions integers \(>0\).',r'For admissible positive-order classical elliptic operators, \(\rho(0)=0\): the noncommutative residue of a pseudodifferential projection vanishes. At other integers, the exact criterion is \(\operatorname{Res}(\Pi P^{-k})=0\); it is not permissible to erase this condition for arbitrary classical pseudodifferential \(P\). The blanket nonpositive-integer assertion can fail even for differential operators: in odd dimensions, negative-integer poles of the absolute zeta or eta functions can give nonzero cut differences. Additional parity or residue-vanishing hypotheses are needed.',r'\citet[Theorem 4.3 and Remark 4.5]{ponge2006spectral} verify zero-value cut independence. \citet[\S4]{loyamoroianu2012singularities} verify admissible negative poles for Dirac-type differential operators in odd dimensions; together with the cut-difference identity these rule out the unrestricted extension. An independent explicit check in \hyperref[dev:1981-negative-cut]{the spectral verification} constructs an invertible massive Dirac operator on the flat three-torus: its two cut zeta values at \(-1\) are \(\mp i\pi^2\mu^4\). The positive-integer hint conflicts with the displayed range and remains a source ambiguity.',('wodzicki1984local','ponge2006spectral','loyamoroianu2012singularities'),cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='high',reconstruction='The valid zero-value theorem is separated from the false general extension. Regularity of a cut zeta function at a negative integer does not imply equality of its values for different cuts.',status='Cut independence at zero is established. The stated extension to every nonpositive integer is false without further restrictions, including within differential-operator settings.')
item(559,'P001025','method','The shifted-zeta polynomial and its missing powers',r'For order one, equation (20) gives a binomial sum plus \[t^k\sum_{\mu=1}^n\frac{(-1)^\mu\delta_\mu}{\mu\binom{k+\mu}{k}},\] without \(t^\mu\) in its summands.',r'For an invertible elliptic differential operator \(A\) of order one on a closed \(n\)-manifold, with a fixed admissible cut and a sufficiently small scalar shift preserving invertibility and the cut, write \(\delta_\mu=\operatorname*{res}_{s=\mu}\zeta_A(s)\). Then for \(k\ge0\), \[\begin{split}\zeta_{A+t}(-k)={}&\sum_{\nu=0}^{k}\binom{k}{\nu}t^\nu\zeta_A(\nu-k)\\&+\sum_{\mu=1}^{n}\frac{(-1)^\mu\delta_\mu\,t^{k+\mu}}{\mu\binom{k+\mu}{k}}.\end{split}\] Kernel terms require separate bookkeeping when invertibility is dropped.',r'The correction follows by expanding \((A+t)^{-s}\) and taking the limit at \(s=-k\); the coefficient is independently checked symbolically in the included code. Wodzicki’s local-invariant programme is the historical context, but a direct published equation-by-equation match to (20) was not read. The literal omission is already contradicted by setting \(t=0\) when a residue is nonzero.',('wodzicki1982spectral','wodzicki1984local'),cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The complete corrected coefficient derivation is included as an annotation in the development file, not claimed as a new theorem of spectral theory.',status='The corrected local shift identity is verified under these hypotheses; the notebook formula without the additional powers is false.')
item(559,'P001025','remark','The Mahler analogy and the unspecified p-adic continuation',r'The first finite sum is called a Mahler transform; an automorphic function for it is said to continue to a p-adic domain.',r'Mahler’s classical theorem expands a continuous \(f:\mathbb Z_p\to\mathbb Q_p\) in the binomial basis, with coefficients tending to zero. To turn the spectral shift identity into a p-adic interpolation statement one would need a specified p-adic coefficient field, sequence, continuity/congruence estimates and meaning of automorphy. None is supplied here.',r'\citet{mahler1958interpolation} is a genuine antecedent for the binomial basis; the further automorphic/p-adic sentence cannot be matched to a precise theorem from the notes. No general p-adic continuation of spectral zeta values is asserted.',('mahler1958interpolation',),cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='low',status='The Mahler basis theorem is established; the proposed spectral/automorphic continuation is not identifiable from the recorded data.')
